window.PSI_INDEX=[{"u":"trastorns.html","t":"Conditions and disorders","h":"List of conditions · Generalised anxiety disorder · Panic disorder and agoraphobia · Social anxiety disorder · Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) · Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) · Depression · Bipolar disorder · Eating disorders · Schizophrenia and psychotic disorders · Borderline personality disorder · ADHD (Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder) · Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) · Addictions and problematic use · Insomnia and sleep problems · Grief and loss · Self-harm and suicidal thoughts · Specific phobias · Complex PTSD and ongoing trauma · Health anxiety (hypochondria) · Other sleep disorders (apnoea, sleepiness, nightmares) · Eating disorders across the lifespan","x":"Psychoeducation Conditions and disorders Plain-language sheets: what they are, how to recognise them, which myths exist and when to ask for help. This is not a diagnosis Nobody can diagnose themselves by reading a website. If you recognise yourself in a sheet, talk to a professional. Search for a condition All Anxiety and related Mood Trauma and grief Eating Psychosis Personality Neurodevelopment Addictions Sleep Risk and crisis List of conditions 🌊 Anxiety and related Generalised anxiety disorder Excessive worry that is hard to control, on most days, for months. 💨 Anxiety and related Panic disorder and agoraphobia Unexpected panic attacks and fear that they will happen again. 🫥 Anxiety and related Social anxiety disorder Intense fear of being judged or embarrassed in social situations. 🔁 Anxiety and related Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) Intrusive thoughts (obsessions) and rituals (compulsions) that briefly relieve distress. ⚡ Trauma and grief Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) Lasting reactions after a traumatic experience. 🌧️ Mood Depression Persistent sadness or loss of interest that affects daily life. 🎢 Mood Bipolar disorder Alternating episodes of elevated mood (mania/hypomania) and depression. 🍽️ Eating Eating disorders Anorexia, bulimia and binge-eating disorder: a troubled relationship with food and the body. 🌀 Psychosis Schizophrenia and psychotic disorders Changes in how reality is perceived: hallucinations, delusions and disorganised thinking. 🎭 Personality Borderline personality disorder Intense emotional instability, in relationships and in one’s self-image. ⚡ Neurodevelopment ADHD (Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder) Persistent difficulties with attention, hyperactivity and/or impulsivity. 🧩 Neurodevelopment Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) A different way of communicating, relating and perceiving the world. 🔗 Addictions Addictions and problematic use Loss of control over substance use or behaviours (gambling, screens...). 🌙 Sleep Insomnia and sleep problems Difficulty sleeping that affects daily life. 🕯️ Trauma and grief Grief and loss A natural process of reacting to a loss, which may need support. 🤝 Risk and crisis Self-harm and suicidal thoughts If you are thinking of harming yourself right now, you are not alone: ask for help now. 🕷️ Anxiety and related Specific phobias Intense, out-of-proportion fear of a specific thing or situation (animals, heights, blood, flying...). 🧩 Trauma and grief Complex PTSD and ongoing trauma Effects of repeated or prolonged trauma, often from childhood: beyond memories, it affects how you see yourself and relate to others. 🩺 Anxiety and related Health anxiety (hypochondria) Persistent worry about having or being able to develop a serious illness, despite medical checks. 😴 Sleep Other sleep disorders (apnoea, sleepiness, nightmares) It isn’t all insomnia: apnoea, hypersomnia, narcolepsy, nightmares and parasomnias also affect rest. 🍎 Eating Eating disorders across the lifespan How eating disorders can show up in childhood, adolescence, adulthood and older age. We found no results. Try another term.","k":"p"},{"u":"eines.html","t":"Tools for everyday life","h":"🫁 Guided breathing · 🌿 5-4-3-2-1 grounding · 💆 Progressive muscle relaxation · 📓 Mood log · 🧠 Thought diary (CBT) · Previous entries · 🧭 My plan · 🌱 More CBT tools · 📦 My data · 🫥 Social anxiety","x":"Self-help Tools for everyday life Short exercises based on well-known techniques. They are a support, not a treatment. Please note If an exercise makes you uncomfortable or feel worse, stop it. If you are in crisis, go to urgent help . 🫁 Guided breathing Follow the circle. Choose a pattern and breathe through your nose if you can. Pattern Calming: in 4 · out 6 Box: 4 · 4 · 4 · 4 4-7-8 Ready? Press start whenever you are ready. Start 🌿 5-4-3-2-1 grounding Helps bring you back to the present when anxiety or thoughts overwhelm you. 5 things you see Look around you and name, quietly or in your head, five things you can see. Previous Next 💆 Progressive muscle relaxation Tense each muscle group for about 5 seconds and let go for 10. Do it sitting or lying down. Ready? Press start and it will guide you step by step. Start 📓 Mood log Stored only in your browser. No one else sees it. How are you feeling now? 😞 🙁 😐 🙂 😄 Note (optional) Save Download (CSV) Delete everything 🧠 Thought diary (CBT) Name the thought, look at the evidence and find a more balanced version. 1. Situation 2. Automatic thought 3. Emotion and intensity (0-100) 4. Evidence for the thought 5. Evidence against it 6. A more balanced alternative thought Save Delete entries Previous entries 🧭 My plan Five questions about the last two weeks and a proposal of small steps, with links to the tools. Make my plan 🌱 More CBT tools Behavioural activation for depression, sleep diary and panic log. Open the tools 📦 My data Print or download your records to take to your appointment, make backups and create reminders. Open 🫥 Social anxiety Specific CBT tools: exposure ladder, social thought record and behavioural experiments. Open the tools","k":"p"},{"u":"eines.html#respiracio","t":"Guided breathing","h":"","x":"Follow the circle. Choose a pattern and breathe through your nose if you can.","k":"e"},{"u":"eines.html#grounding","t":"5-4-3-2-1 grounding","h":"","x":"Helps bring you back to the present when anxiety or thoughts overwhelm you.","k":"e"},{"u":"eines.html#relaxacio","t":"Progressive muscle relaxation","h":"","x":"Tense each muscle group for about 5 seconds and let go for 10. Do it sitting or lying down.","k":"e"},{"u":"eines.html#anim","t":"Mood log","h":"","x":"Stored only in your browser. No one else sees it.","k":"e"},{"u":"eines.html#pensaments","t":"Thought diary (CBT)","h":"","x":"Name the thought, look at the evidence and find a more balanced version.","k":"e"},{"u":"eines-tcc.html","t":"Behavioural activation, sleep diary and panic log","h":"🌱 Behavioural activation (depression) · Your activities · 🌙 Sleep diary · 💨 Panic log","x":"← All tools CBT · self-help Behavioural activation, sleep diary and panic log Three cognitive behavioural therapy tools for depression, insomnia and anxiety. Stored only in your browser. A support, not a treatment If you have felt unwell for weeks, have had repeated panic attacks or have not slept well for months, talk to your doctor or a psychologist. Find help 🌱 Behavioural activation (depression) When you are feeling low, the urge is to do less, and that makes mood worse. The idea is the opposite: act first, and motivation will follow . Plan small activities, then note how much pleasure and sense of achievement they really gave you. Activity (small and specific) Type Self-care Social Movement Tasks and responsibilities Pleasure and leisure Pleasure I think it will give: 3 /10 Sense of achievement: 3 /10 Plan Your activities Delete activities Tips Start with things that take 5-10 minutes. Pick some that give you pleasure and others that give you a sense of achievement. Don’t wait until you feel like it: plan the moment (day and time). If low mood lasts for weeks or you have thoughts of harming yourself, get help now: 024 or 112. 🌙 Sleep diary Note it down every morning (2 minutes). With one or two weeks of data, you and the professional can spot patterns. Night of How did I sleep? 1 · Very bad 2 · Bad 3 · Okay 4 · Good 5 · Very good Bedtime Wake-up time Minutes until I fell asleep Minutes awake during the night Yesterday… Caffeine in the afternoon or evening Alcohol Screen in bed Long nap Exercise during the day Note (optional) Save Delete diary Sleep hygiene Regular schedule (weekends too) · daylight in the morning · less caffeine after noon · no alcohol to get to sleep · screens out of bed · if you don’t fall asleep in about 20 minutes, get up and do something calm until you feel sleepy. The bed is for sleeping. For persistent insomnia, CBT-I is the recommended treatment: read the sheet . 💨 Panic log During a panic attack The symptoms are very unpleasant but not dangerous , and they pass: they usually peak within about 10 minutes. Breathe out slowly (in for 4 seconds, out for 6), let the sensation pass without fighting it and remind yourself: “this is panic, it will pass”. If it is your first time or you have chest pain that doesn’t go away, call 112. Note it down afterwards, once you are calmer. Seeing the pattern helps reduce the fear. Peak intensity: 60 /100 Approximate duration (minutes) Symptoms Palpitations Breathlessness or chest tightness Sweating Trembling Dizziness Tingling Nausea Feeling unreal Fear of losing control Fear of dying Where were you and what was happening? What did you fear would happen? What I did (and what happened?) Save Delete log To learn more Read the panic disorder sheet and try the guided breathing . Take it to your appointment You can print or download these records to show your doctor or psychologist.","k":"p"},{"u":"eines-tcc.html#activacio","t":"Behavioural activation (depression)","h":"","x":"When you are feeling low, the urge is to do less, and that makes mood worse. The idea is the opposite: act first, and motivation will follow . Plan small activities, then note how much pleasure and sense of achievement they really gave you.","k":"e"},{"u":"eines-tcc.html#son","t":"Sleep diary","h":"","x":"Note it down every morning (2 minutes). With one or two weeks of data, you and the professional can spot patterns.","k":"e"},{"u":"eines-tcc.html#panic","t":"Panic log","h":"","x":"Note it down afterwards, once you are calmer. Seeing the pattern helps reduce the fear.","k":"e"},{"u":"ansietat-social-eines.html","t":"Social anxiety: CBT tools","h":"🪜 1. Exposure ladder · 🧠 2. Social thought record · Previous entries · 🔬 3. Behavioural experiments · Before · Your experiments · 🤖 AI assistant (experimental)","x":"← All tools Cognitive behavioural therapy Social anxiety: CBT tools Three tools to work on the fear of being judged: ranking what you avoid, questioning thoughts and testing them in practice. A support, not a treatment They work better alongside a psychologist. Everything is stored only in your browser. If you feel overwhelmed, stop and see urgent help . 🪜 1. Exposure ladder List social situations you avoid or find hard, and rate the fear from 0 (none) to 100 (maximum). Start with the low scores and work up gradually. Repeat each situation until the fear goes down. Situation Expected fear: 40 /100 Add to the ladder Tip: choose a step that gives you about 30-50 fear, not the hardest one. Staying in the situation until the anxiety drops teaches you that you can tolerate it. Delete the ladder 🧠 2. Social thought record For after a situation (or before). Put your predictions to the test. Social situation What I think will happen or what they thought of me How much I believe it now: 60 % Distortions I recognise (optional) Mind reading (I think I know what they are thinking) Catastrophising (I see it as worse than it is) Personalisation (everything is about me) Labelling (“I’m a failure”) Mental filter (I only see the negative) Should statements (rigid demands) Evidence for Evidence against More realistic alternative thought Now, how much I believe it: 40 % Save Delete entries Previous entries 🔬 3. Behavioural experiments A test of your prediction. First write what you think will happen, then do it and note what really happened. Before What I will do (specific and small) What I think will happen Expected anxiety: 60 /100 Try not to use “safety behaviours” (avoiding eye contact, looking at your phone, repeating your script). They make us believe we only cope thanks to them. Plan experiment Your experiments Delete experiments 🤖 AI assistant (experimental) Practise social conversations and reframe thoughts with a model that runs on your device. Try the assistant What if it’s too hard? That’s normal. Take smaller steps or talk to a psychologist: CBT with exposure is the best-evidenced treatment for social anxiety. Read the sheet · Find help","k":"p"},{"u":"ansietat-social-eines.html#exposicio","t":"Exposure ladder","h":"","x":"List social situations you avoid or find hard, and rate the fear from 0 (none) to 100 (maximum). Start with the low scores and work up gradually. Repeat each situation until the fear goes down.","k":"e"},{"u":"ansietat-social-eines.html#pensaments-soc","t":"Social thought record","h":"","x":"For after a situation (or before). Put your predictions to the test.","k":"e"},{"u":"ansietat-social-eines.html#experiments","t":"Behavioural experiments","h":"","x":"A test of your prediction. First write what you think will happen, then do it and note what really happened.","k":"e"},{"u":"ansietat-social-assistent.html","t":"CBT assistant with AI","h":"1. Activate the assistant · 2. Choose what you want to do","x":"← Social anxiety tools Experimental · optional CBT assistant with AI Practise social conversations, reframe thoughts and prepare behavioural experiments with an AI model that runs on your device. It is not a therapist It is an experimental AI and can be wrong, especially in Catalan. It does not diagnose or replace a professional. If you are in crisis, call 024 or 112 . 1. Activate the assistant Nothing is downloaded until you ask. Conversations are not stored or sent anywhere : they stay on the screen and disappear when you close the page. Where do you want it to run? In the browser (recommended): private and nothing to install. Needs a recent desktop Chrome or Edge (WebGPU). Local Ollama : if you already have it installed on your computer. Allows better models. Conversation language Español English Català For technical reasons, English works better Small AI models that run on your device understand and write English (and Spanish) noticeably better than Catalan, where they tend to make mistakes or lose the thread. That is why the assistant chats in English by default. The rest of the website is available in your language. Model Llama 3.2 · 3B (~2.3 GB) · recommended, heavier Gemma 2 · 2B (~1.5 GB) · balanced Llama 3.2 · 1B (~0.9 GB) · light, more limited When you activate it, the library from jsDelivr and the model from Hugging Face will be downloaded (third-party services, which may log your IP address). It is stored in the browser cache. Start Ollama allowing this website and download a model. In the terminal: OLLAMA_ORIGINS=https://francescsolsonatehas.github.io ollama serve ollama pull llama3.2 Detect Ollama Ollama model The website will only talk to your Ollama at localhost ; nothing leaves your computer. Some browsers (e.g. Safari) block this connection from an https website. I understand this is an experimental AI, not a therapist, and that it may say incorrect things. I accept the download described. Activate the assistant Tick the box above to be able to turn the assistant on. 2. Choose what you want to do Mode Practise a social conversation Reframe a thought (Socratic questions) Prepare a behavioural experiment Scene Work colleague (coffee) Neighbour (lift) Shop assistant Stranger at the stop Before a meeting Job interview Difficulty Easy Medium Hard Start Clear Thank you for saying it. How you feel matters. This tool cannot help you with this, but there are people who can: call 024 (free, 24 h) now, or 112 if there is immediate danger. If you can, don’t stay alone. More resources Your message Send Stop Finish and ask for feedback Better with a professional This assistant is a complement for practising. CBT works best with a psychologist. Find help","k":"p"},{"u":"cribratges.html","t":"Screening: PHQ-9 and GAD-7","h":"","x":"Indicative Screening: PHQ-9 and GAD-7 Short questionnaires that measure symptoms of depression and anxiety over the last two weeks. This is not a diagnosis Results do not replace a clinical assessment. Your answers are not sent anywhere: they are calculated on your device. PHQ-9 · Mood GAD-7 · Anxiety See result Delete answers The items are an English-language presentation of the PHQ-9 and GAD-7 scales (Pfizer Inc., free to use). They must be interpreted by a professional.","k":"p"},{"u":"ajuda.html","t":"Find help","h":"Helplines · Emergencies · Suicide prevention line · ANAR – children and teenagers · Salut Respon (Catalonia) · Telèfon de l’Esperança (Hope Line) · Public healthcare in Catalonia · Private care and associations","x":"Resources Find help Phone numbers and services for different moments. If in doubt, call: no one will judge you. Urgent: danger to you or to someone else Don’t wait. Call now. Helplines Emergencies Immediate risk to life or physical safety. 112 Suicide prevention line Free, confidential and open 24 h every day. For anyone thinking of harming themselves and for relatives. 024 ANAR – children and teenagers Confidential, free help for under-18s, 24 h. Also on 116 111. Relatives and schools: 600 50 51 52. 900 20 20 10 Salut Respon (Catalonia) Telephone health service 24 h; directs you to health services. 061 Telèfon de l’Esperança (Hope Line) Emotional support, someone to listen and keep you company (Barcelona). 93 414 48 48 Public healthcare in Catalonia CAP (primary care centre): the family doctor is the first step. They can assess, advise and refer to mental health services. CSMA / CSMIJ: Adult and Child and Youth Mental Health Centres. Access is normally by referral. CAS: Centres for addiction care and follow-up. Hospital emergency departments: if there is serious, immediate risk. Private care and associations Official College of Psychology of Catalonia : you can check that a psychologist is registered. Salut Mental Catalunya : federation of associations of people with mental health problems and their families. Canal Salut : the Government of Catalonia’s official health portal. Outside Catalonia In the rest of Spain, 024 is free and works nationwide. Outside Spain, check the local emergency service. Phone numbers and services may change. Check them on official websites before relying on a number.","k":"p"},{"u":"familiars.html","t":"For family members and carers","h":"What you can say · What it is best to avoid · How to help in practice · And how are you?","x":"For those who support someone For family members and carers Supporting someone who is suffering psychologically is not easy. You deserve support too. What you can say “I’m here and I’m listening.” “That must be really hard. Thank you for telling me.” “You’re not a burden to me.” “Shall we look for help together?” What it is best to avoid “Cheer up”, “it’s not that bad”, “you have to be strong”. Comparing their suffering with other people’s. Judging, blaming or joking about their condition. Promising to keep secrets if their life is at risk. How to help in practice Offer to go with them to their first appointment with the doctor or psychologist. Help with basic routines: sleeping, eating, going for a walk. Respect their pace: sometimes help is just being there. Read up with the condition sheets . If you suspect they are thinking of harming themselves Ask them directly and calmly. Asking does not increase the risk. If there is immediate danger, don’t leave them alone and call 112 . For guidance, 024 also supports relatives. And how are you? Caring is also tiring. Make time for yourself, talk to someone you trust and consider family associations, where there are people who have been through similar situations. Help resources Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"p"},{"u":"parlar-ne.html","t":"How to talk about it","h":"With a relative or friend · Before you start · Phrases to start with · If the reaction isn’t what you expected · With your GP (CAP) · What to bring · How to start the appointment · What can happen · Useful questions · At work · If you need sick leave · If you want to keep working with adjustments · Who to talk to","x":"Practical guide How to talk about it Putting words to what is happening to you is a big step. Here are ideas and phrases to start with family, your GP and at work. It doesn’t have to be perfect You don’t need to have it all figured out or find the exact words. You can start by just saying “I’m not feeling well”. With family and friends With your doctor At work With a relative or friend Before you start Pick someone you trust, not necessarily the first person who comes to mind. Pick a calm moment, without rush or distractions. It can be while walking or by message, if face to face is hard. Decide what you want to ask for: listening , company , help finding a professional … Phrases to start with “I haven’t been feeling well lately and I wanted to tell you. I don’t need you to fix it, just to listen.” “I’ve had trouble sleeping for a while and I’m very anxious. I’d like to ask for help and it would help if you came with me.” “I don’t quite know what’s wrong, but I don’t feel like myself. Can you help me find who I can ask?” If the reaction isn’t what you expected Sometimes people downplay it (“that’s just you”) or get scared. Often it isn’t a lack of affection, they just don’t know what to say. You can tell them: “It hurts to hear that; what helps me is…”. And remember you can try someone else or go straight to a professional. With your GP (CAP) The family doctor is the usual way into the public system. They have little time, so preparing helps a lot. What to bring A list of what you feel , since when and how it affects you (sleep, work, relationships, appetite). Any medicines or supplements you take. Family medical history, if you know it. Records from this website (mood, sleep, panic…): you can print or download them . How to start the appointment State the main reason right away, in one sentence. For example: “For a few weeks I’ve been feeling very low and without energy, and it’s hard to go to work.” “I have panic attacks and I’m avoiding more and more things.” “I’ve been sleeping very little for months and I’m exhausted.” If you are thinking of harming yourself Say it out loud. It will be treated as a priority and you won’t be judged. If it is urgent, call 024 or 112 . What can happen They will ask about your symptoms and, sometimes, give a short questionnaire. They may ask for a blood test to rule out physical causes (for example thyroid problems or anaemia). They may propose follow-up, treatment or a referral to a psychologist or a mental health centre (CSMA). Useful questions “What options do I have? What are the pros and cons of each?” “How long will it take before I notice an improvement?” “When shall we meet again?” “If I get worse, what do I do or who do I call?” You can bring someone with you. If you don’t feel listened to, you have the right to ask for another appointment or a second opinion. At work Deciding whether to tell your employer, and who, is your decision. You are not obliged to disclose your diagnosis to your employer. If you need sick leave Your doctor handles the sick leave. The sick note that reaches your employer does not include the diagnosis. You can simply say: “My doctor has signed me off for health reasons”. If you want to keep working with adjustments You can ask for reasonable changes: flexible hours, working from home some days, sharing out tasks, breaks, or reducing a temporary workload. Some example phrases: “I’m going through a difficult time with my health and I’d like to talk about some temporary adjustments so I can keep working.” “A more regular schedule would help me for a few weeks.” Who to talk to Your direct manager or human resources, if you trust them. The company’s occupational health and safety service, which has a duty of confidentiality. Union or safety representatives, if you have them. If the distress comes from work (harassment, overload, discrimination), note down dates and facts and ask for advice. Your health matters Looking after yourself is not failing at work. If you can’t go on, talking to a professional is the first step. Find help For family Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"p"},{"u":"joves.html","t":"You are not alone in this","h":"What is normal and what is less so · How to tell someone · Things that can help from today · Social media, screens and pressure · If you are a friend of someone who is struggling · Phone numbers · ANAR · Suicide prevention · Emergencies","x":"For young people You are not alone in this Studies, friends, social media, family, working out who you are… Feeling bad now and then is human. Here is clear information to help you understand yourself and know who to ask for help. If you want to hurt yourself right now or fear for your life Call 900 20 20 10 (ANAR, free) , 024 or 112 now. You can also tell a trusted adult. Right now. What is normal and what is less so Everyone has rubbish days: an exam, a row, a disappointment. That happens. It is worth asking for help when, for more than two weeks , any of these happens: You feel sad, empty or irritable almost every day. You have lost interest in things you used to enjoy. You sleep much more or much less than usual, or you have lost your appetite (or eat compulsively). You find it hard to concentrate and your grades or work are slipping. You avoid friends or going out, or you have intense fear of social situations. You have anxiety or panic attacks. You hurt yourself, or you think it would be better not to be here. If you recognise yourself, it doesn’t mean you are “crazy”. It means you may deserve some support. How to tell someone It’s hard, but it’s a very brave step. You can start with: A trusted adult : a parent, guardians, a teacher, your school tutor, a coach, a relative. Your paediatrician or GP (at the CAP). You can book an appointment for what you feel. At school: your tutor or the school counsellor / educational psychologist can help. 900 20 20 10 (ANAR) is free and confidential, for under-18s. You can say: “I haven’t been feeling well for a while and I don’t know what to do. Can you help me?” . That is enough. More ideas in the “How to talk about it” guide . Things that can help from today Sleep: regular schedules and phone out of bed. Sleep changes mood a lot. Sleep diary Move: a walk, dancing, sport. A little every day is worth more than a lot on a single day. Breathe and pause: guided breathing or 5-4-3-2-1 when anxiety rises. Putting a name to what you feel: the mood log and the thought diary . Don’t cut yourself off completely: a message to a friend is already a lot. Social media, screens and pressure What you see on social media is a selection. Don’t compare yourself with other people’s “perfect life”. If a platform or account makes you feel worse, you can mute it, unfollow it or take a break. If someone is harassing you, threatening you or sharing photos of you, it is not your fault. Keep screenshots, block them and tell an adult. You can call ANAR (900 20 20 10). If you are a friend of someone who is struggling Listen without judging and without minimising (“it’s not that bad”). Ask directly and calmly how they are. Asking doesn’t make anything worse. Don’t keep it secret if someone wants to harm themselves: tell an adult. It isn’t betraying them: it’s caring for them. If there is immediate danger, don’t leave them alone and call 112. Phone numbers ANAR Free, confidential help for under-18s, 24 h. 900 20 20 10 Suicide prevention Free, 24 h, for any age. 024 Emergencies If there is immediate danger. 112 This doesn’t define you Going through a hard time doesn’t make you weak or less important. And with help, most people get better. More resources Understanding what I feel Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"p"},{"u":"pla.html","t":"My plan","h":"📝 The last two weeks · 🧭 Your plan · How you have been · Your steps","x":"Self-help My plan Answer five questions about the last two weeks and you will get a proposal of small steps, with links to this website's tools. You can swap any steps that don't suit you. A guide, not a diagnosis The plan is only a guide and does not replace a professional. Your answers and plan are stored only in your browser. 📝 The last two weeks How often has this happened over the last two weeks? There are no right or wrong answers. 1. Have you slept badly: found it hard to fall asleep, woken up often or too early? Not at all Several days More than half the days Nearly every day 2. Have you felt sad or had no desire to do things you used to enjoy? Not at all Several days More than half the days Nearly every day 3. Have you felt nervous, restless or full of worries you can't switch off? Not at all Several days More than half the days Nearly every day 4. Have you felt overwhelmed, with too much on your plate (work, studies, home)? Not at all Several days More than half the days Nearly every day 5. Have you felt lonely or avoided being around other people? Not at all Several days More than half the days Nearly every day Optional: have you thought that it would be better not to be here, or thought about harming yourself? No Several days Often See my plan 🧭 Your plan Thank you for saying so Thinking about harming yourself is more common than it seems, and it can be talked about. Call 024 now (free, 24 hours) or, if you are in immediate danger, 112 . You don't have to go through this alone. It's worth talking to someone Your answers suggest you have been struggling for some days. A self-help plan helps, but it isn't enough for everything: talk to your GP or a psychologist. How to do it · Find help How you have been Your steps Pick the ones that seem possible this week. If a step doesn't suit you, swap it for another. Add a reminder to your calendar Answer again Delete the plan If you are under 18, also take a look at the page for young people . If the difficulties last more than two weeks or affect your daily life, seek professional help .","k":"p"},{"u":"meves-dades.html","t":"My data and reminders","h":"📦 What you have stored · 🖨️ To take to your appointment · 💾 Backup · ⏰ Gentle reminders for your calendar · 🗑️ Delete everything","x":"← Tools Your data My data and reminders Everything you note in the tools is stored only in your browser. Here you can print it, make a backup or set up reminders for your calendar. 📦 What you have stored The data lives only in this browser and device. If you clear the browser’s data, it will be lost: that is why a backup is a good idea. 🖨️ To take to your appointment Generates a readable summary to show your doctor or psychologist. You can print it, save it as a PDF or download it as text. What would you like to include? Show the summary Print or save as PDF Download (.txt) 💾 Backup Download a file with all your data, or restore an earlier one (for example on another device). Download backup (.json) Import a backup When importing… Add to what I already have (without duplicating) Replace what I have Import ⏰ Gentle reminders for your calendar This website does not send notifications. You can download a calendar file (.ics) and open it with your calendar (Google, Apple, Outlook…) so that it reminds you. Reminder My plan Mood log Guided breathing Sleep diary Planned activity (behavioural activation) Thought diary Exposure step (social anxiety) Time Frequency Every day Monday to Friday Once a week For how many weeks 2 4 8 12 Download reminder (.ics) The file only contains a friendly title and a link to this website, never your data. 🗑️ Delete everything Permanently deletes all the tool data in this browser. This cannot be undone. Delete all data","k":"p"},{"u":"meves-dades.html#imprimir","t":"To take to your appointment","h":"","x":"Generates a readable summary to show your doctor or psychologist. You can print it, save it as a PDF or download it as text.","k":"e"},{"u":"fonts-i-revisio.html","t":"Who reviews it and where the information comes from","h":"Professional review · How the content was produced · Reference sources · Updates and errors · What this website does not do","x":"Transparency Who reviews it and where the information comes from A mental health website should be able to explain where what it says comes from. Here is, without sugar-coating, how it was made and what is still pending. Professional review Pending This website has not yet been reviewed by any licensed mental health professional. Until it is, treat all content as general information and always consult a professional. [Pending: name, professional registration number, specialty and review date of the reviewing professional(s).] Once there is a professional reviewer, the condition sheets, the tools and questionnaires, the phone numbers and resources, and the translations will be reviewed. Each sheet and guide will say whether it has been reviewed and when. How the content was produced The texts were written with the help of an artificial intelligence tool, based on the public sources listed below. They are a popular-science summary: they contain no original research or clinical experience. The Spanish and English versions are translations made with the same tool, without review by a translator or a professional. The PHQ-9 and GAD-7 questionnaires are adaptations of the original scales, which are free to use, and are not officially validated versions in any of the three languages. The self-help tools are based on well-known cognitive behavioural therapy techniques. They are a support, not a treatment. Reference sources World Health Organization – Mental health Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) NICE – Mental health clinical guidelines GuíaSalud – Clinical practice guidelines of the Spanish National Health System ICD-11 (WHO) and DSM-5-TR (American Psychiatric Association) , as reference classifications Kroenke K, Spitzer RL, Williams JBW. The PHQ-9: validity of a brief depression severity measure. J Gen Intern Med . 2001;16(9):606-613. Spitzer RL, Kroenke K, Williams JBW, Löwe B. A brief measure for assessing generalized anxiety disorder: the GAD-7. Arch Intern Med . 2006;166(10):1092-1097. Updates and errors Content last updated: November 2026. Phone numbers and services can change: always check them on the official websites. If you spot a mistake or information that needs updating, see the contact details in the legal notice . What this website does not do It does not diagnose or recommend medication or treatments. It does not replace seeing a healthcare professional. It does not collect personal data: here is how we explain it . Find professional help Legal and medical notice","k":"p"},{"u":"avis-legal.html","t":"Legal and medical notice","h":"Medical notice · Screening · Content and liability · Intellectual property · Owner","x":"Legal and medical notice Medical notice This website is for information and education only. It does not offer diagnosis, treatment or personalised medical or psychological advice and does not replace a consultation with a qualified health professional. Never ignore a professional’s advice or stop a treatment because of something you read here. In an emergency, call 112. If you have suicidal thoughts, call 024. Screening The PHQ-9 and GAD-7 questionnaires are indicative tools. A result neither confirms nor rules out any disorder. Content and liability Every effort is made for the content to be accurate and based on recognised sources, but it is provided “as is”, without warranties. The owner is not responsible for decisions made on the basis of the information on the website. Intellectual property The Inter and Poppins typefaces are distributed under the SIL Open Font License 1.1. Owner [Fill in here the name or company name, tax ID and contact email of the website owner, as required by the Spanish LSSI-CE.]","k":"p"},{"u":"privacitat.html","t":"Privacy and cookies","h":"Data that is stored · Cookies and third parties · AI assistant (optional) · Rights (GDPR)","x":"Privacy and cookies In short This website does not collect personal data, has no user accounts and uses no cookies or third-party analytics. Data that is stored The tools (mood log, thought diary, behavioural activation, sleep diary, panic log, social anxiety tools and the personal plan) and your preferences (dark mode, text size) are stored only in your browser using its local storage (localStorage). Nothing is sent to any server. You can delete them at any time from the tools themselves or by clearing the site's data in your browser. Screening results are calculated on your device and are not stored. Backups, printable summaries and reminders (.ics) are generated in your browser and downloaded to your device; they never pass through any server. If you share these files, you decide where they go. Cookies and third parties No cookies are used. The typefaces are served from this same site, so no requests are made to Google Fonts or other third parties. Hosting is on GitHub Pages, which may log technical connection data (such as the IP address) under its own privacy policy. AI assistant (optional) Only if you activate the assistant on the social anxiety page does your browser download the WebLLM library from jsDelivr and the model from Hugging Face ; these services may log technical data such as your IP address. The model runs on your device and conversations are neither sent nor stored . If you choose Ollama, the website only connects to localhost on your computer. Rights (GDPR) Since no personal data is processed, there is no data on which to exercise rights. If this changes, this page will be updated. Shared device? Delete your records from the Tools section when you are done.","k":"p"},{"u":"trastorns/ansietat-generalitzada.html","t":"Generalised anxiety disorder","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Anxiety and related 🌊 Generalised anxiety disorder Excessive worry that is hard to control, on most days, for months. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Everyone feels anxious from time to time. When worry is constant, out of proportion and gets in the way of work, relationships or rest, it may be generalised anxiety disorder (GAD). Common symptoms Constant worry about many things (health, work, family, money) Restlessness, feeling “on edge” Tiredness and difficulty concentrating Muscle tension, headaches or digestive problems Difficulty falling asleep or unrefreshing sleep Irritability Myths and facts Myth: You just need to “stop thinking about it”. Fact: Anxiety is not a matter of willpower. There are effective treatments and skills that can be learned. Myth: Anxiety is always bad. Fact: It is a natural response that protects us. It becomes a problem when it is intense, constant and out of proportion. When to see a professional If worry takes up much of your day, you find it hard to function normally, or it has lasted more than six months, talk to your family doctor or a psychologist. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Clinical guidelines recommend cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) as the first option; it teaches ways to manage worry. In some cases a professional may also consider medication. The decision is always up to a health professional. Tips for family and friends Listen without minimising (“it’s not that bad”). Avoid agreeing with every fear, but don’t push them to face everything at once either. Encourage them to ask for help and offer to go with them to the first appointment. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/trastorn-de-panic.html","t":"Panic disorder and agoraphobia","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Anxiety and related 💨 Panic disorder and agoraphobia Unexpected panic attacks and fear that they will happen again. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is A panic attack is an intense wave of fear that peaks within a few minutes. When attacks keep happening and cause constant fear of having another, it is called panic disorder. It can come with avoiding places or situations (agoraphobia). Common symptoms Palpitations, breathlessness, chest tightness Dizziness, tingling, sweating or trembling Feeling unreal or losing control Fear of “going mad” or dying (even though the attack itself is not dangerous) Avoiding places where attacks have happened Myths and facts Myth: A panic attack can cause a heart attack. Fact: The attack is very unpleasant but not dangerous. If it is your first time or you have doubts, always see a doctor to rule out physical causes. When to see a professional If you have had a panic attack for the first time, see a doctor to rule out physical causes. If they keep happening or start to limit your life, look for professional help. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? CBT with gradual exposure, breathing techniques and reinterpreting bodily sensations is the best-evidenced treatment. A professional may also consider medication. Tips for family and friends During an attack, stay calm, speak softly and remind them that it will pass. Don’t help them avoid everything: avoidance keeps the problem going. Respect their pace during gradual exposure. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/ansietat-social.html","t":"Social anxiety disorder","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Anxiety and related 🫥 Social anxiety disorder Intense fear of being judged or embarrassed in social situations. CBT tools for social anxiety Exposure hierarchy, thought record and behavioural experiments. Open the tools → Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is It goes beyond shyness. There is a persistent fear of being judged negatively, which leads to avoiding situations such as public speaking, meeting people or eating in front of others. Common symptoms Intense fear before and during social situations Blushing, sweating, trembling, shaky voice Dreading the situation for days beforehand Avoiding situations or enduring them with great distress Self-criticism after an interaction Myths and facts Myth: It’s just shyness. Fact: Shyness is a personality trait. Social anxiety causes significant distress and limits life. When to see a professional When fear stops you studying, working, making friends or taking part in activities you would enjoy. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? CBT focused on thoughts and gradual exposure to feared situations. Medication may also be considered. Tips for family and friends Don’t force them to face situations all at once. Celebrate small steps forward. Don’t tease them or label them “shy”. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/toc.html","t":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD)","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Anxiety and related 🔁 Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) Intrusive thoughts (obsessions) and rituals (compulsions) that briefly relieve distress. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Obsessions are unwanted thoughts, images or urges that cause distress. Compulsions are repeated behaviours or mental acts done to reduce it. It takes up a lot of time and interferes with daily life. Common symptoms Fears of contamination, of mistakes, of harming others or of having “terrible thoughts” Excessive washing, checking, ordering or repeating Need for symmetry or exactness Mental rituals (counting, praying, repeating phrases) Constantly seeking reassurance Myths and facts Myth: OCD means being very tidy. Fact: Being tidy is a trait. OCD causes suffering and is not a preference. Myth: Having intrusive thoughts means you are dangerous. Fact: They are very common. In OCD they carry disproportionate weight, but they say nothing about who you are. When to see a professional When rituals take up more than an hour a day, cause distress or limit your life. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? CBT with exposure and response prevention is the first-line treatment. Serotonin reuptake inhibitors can help in some cases, always by prescription. Tips for family and friends Don’t take part in the rituals or give repeated reassurance: without meaning to, it reinforces them. Show empathy without judging. Help them get access to an OCD specialist. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/estres-posttraumatic.html","t":"Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Trauma and grief ⚡ Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) Lasting reactions after a traumatic experience. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is It can appear after living through or witnessing a threatening event (accident, assault, violence, war, sudden loss). The body and mind stay on “alert” for months or years afterwards. Common symptoms Recurring memories or nightmares Avoiding related people, places or conversations Hypervigilance, being easily startled, irritability Numb emotions or feeling disconnected Guilt or negative thoughts about yourself or the world Myths and facts Myth: Only soldiers get PTSD. Fact: Anyone exposed to trauma can develop it. Myth: You have to “get over it” on your own. Fact: People can recover with the right support and treatment. When to see a professional If, more than a month after a traumatic event, symptoms continue or get worse, or if they affect your life. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Trauma-focused therapies (trauma-focused CBT, EMDR) have the most evidence. They should be delivered by trained professionals. Tips for family and friends Don’t pressure them to talk about what happened. Offer a predictable and safe environment. Look after yourself too: supporting someone with trauma is draining. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/depressio.html","t":"Depression","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Mood 🌧️ Depression Persistent sadness or loss of interest that affects daily life. If you are in danger now Call 112 or 024 (free, 24 h). You don’t have to face this alone. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Depression is more than feeling sad for a few days. It is a mood disorder that affects how you feel, think and act, and lasts weeks or months. Common symptoms Sadness or emptiness most of the day Loss of interest or pleasure in activities Changes in appetite, weight or sleep Tiredness and lack of energy Feelings of guilt or worthlessness Difficulty concentrating or making decisions Thoughts of death or of not wanting to live (see the urgent help section) Myths and facts Myth: It is weakness or lack of willpower. Fact: It is a health problem with biological, psychological and social causes. Myth: If you have a “good” life, you can’t be depressed. Fact: Depression can appear without any obvious reason. When to see a professional If symptoms last more than two weeks and affect your life. If you have thoughts of harming yourself, ask for help immediately (112 / 024). What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Depending on severity: psychotherapy (CBT, behavioural activation, interpersonal therapy), antidepressants or a combination. Exercise, a sleep routine and social support help as a complement. Tips for family and friends Listen without trying to “cheer them up” by force. Suggest small shared activities, without demanding. Take any comment about not wanting to live seriously and ask them directly, calmly. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/trastorn-bipolar.html","t":"Bipolar disorder","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Mood 🎢 Bipolar disorder Alternating episodes of elevated mood (mania/hypomania) and depression. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is It is marked by pronounced changes in mood and energy. Episodes of euphoria or intense irritability alternate with depressive episodes, with periods of stability in between. Common symptoms Periods of high energy, little sleep and lots of activity Grandiose ideas, rapid speech, impulsivity Spending sprees or risky behaviour Depressive periods with sadness and lack of energy Myths and facts Myth: These are normal mood swings. Fact: Bipolar episodes are more intense and longer-lasting and affect functioning. Myth: With medication you no longer need therapy. Fact: Psychoeducation and routine are pillars of treatment. When to see a professional See a psychiatrist if you have noticed periods of unusually high energy and activity or marked mood changes. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Usual treatment combines mood stabilisers prescribed by psychiatry with psychoeducation and regular routines (sleep, schedules). Medication should not be stopped without consulting the professional. Tips for family and friends Learn to recognise the warning signs of a new episode. Avoid arguing about ideas during a manic episode. Help them keep regular sleep schedules. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/trastorns-alimentaris.html","t":"Eating disorders","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Eating 🍽️ Eating disorders Anorexia, bulimia and binge-eating disorder: a troubled relationship with food and the body. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is They are serious mental health problems, not whims or a matter of willpower. They include food restriction, binge eating and compensatory behaviours, and excessive concern about weight and body image. Common symptoms Food restriction or intense fear of gaining weight Binge eating followed by guilt and compensatory behaviours Constant preoccupation with weight and body Avoiding eating in public, ritualising meals Isolation, mood changes, tiredness Myths and facts Myth: They only affect teenage girls. Fact: They can affect people of any age and gender. Myth: It is a fad or a matter of vanity. Fact: They are serious disorders with risks to physical and mental health. When to see a professional The sooner help is sought, the better the outlook. See someone if there is significant weight loss, dizziness, loss of periods or compensatory behaviours. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? It requires a multidisciplinary approach: doctor, psychologist, psychiatrist and dietitian. In teenagers, the family is a key part of treatment. Tips for family and friends Don’t comment on weight or body, for better or for worse. Don’t force them to eat; look for professional help. Keep family meals a calm space. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/esquizofrenia.html","t":"Schizophrenia and psychotic disorders","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Psychosis 🌀 Schizophrenia and psychotic disorders Changes in how reality is perceived: hallucinations, delusions and disorganised thinking. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Psychosis involves a loss of contact with reality. Schizophrenia is one of the best-known psychotic disorders. With appropriate treatment, many people lead full lives. Common symptoms Hearing voices or seeing things others do not perceive Fixed ideas that do not follow logic (delusions) Disorganised thinking and speech Social withdrawal, apathy, lack of motivation Neglecting hygiene or usual activities Myths and facts Myth: Schizophrenia is “split personality”. Fact: It is not: they are completely different conditions. Myth: People with schizophrenia are violent. Fact: The vast majority are not; they are more often victims than aggressors. When to see a professional After a first psychotic episode or a sudden change in behaviour, an urgent psychiatric assessment is needed. Early intervention improves the outlook. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Antipsychotic treatment prescribed by psychiatry, combined with psychoeducation, therapy and social and work support. Tips for family and friends Don’t argue about the content of delusions; be present and calm. Help them stay on their treatment. Look for support groups for families. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/trastorn-limit-personalitat.html","t":"Borderline personality disorder","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Personality 🎭 Borderline personality disorder Intense emotional instability, in relationships and in one’s self-image. If you are in danger now Call 112 or 024 (free, 24 h). You don’t have to face this alone. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is It is marked by very intense emotions that change quickly, fear of abandonment, unstable relationships and impulsivity. It is a treatable disorder with a good outlook given the right support. Common symptoms Intense fear of being abandoned Intense, unstable relationships Rapid mood changes Chronic feeling of emptiness Impulsivity or self-harming behaviours Anger that is hard to control Myths and facts Myth: They are manipulative people. Fact: They often feel enormous emotional pain and use the strategies they have. Myth: They can’t be treated. Fact: Specific therapies such as DBT have good results. When to see a professional If unstable emotions or relationships cause you ongoing distress, or there is self-harm. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Specific psychotherapies: dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT), mentalisation-based therapy, among others. Tips for family and friends Validate their emotions without validating every behaviour. Set clear, kind limits. Look for support for yourself: caring for someone is demanding. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/tdah.html","t":"ADHD (Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder)","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Neurodevelopment ⚡ ADHD (Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder) Persistent difficulties with attention, hyperactivity and/or impulsivity. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is It is a neurodevelopmental disorder that appears in childhood and can continue into adulthood. It is not a lack of upbringing or willpower. Common symptoms Difficulty sustaining attention Frequent distraction, forgetfulness Restlessness, difficulty sitting still Impulsivity Disorganisation and difficulty managing time Myths and facts Myth: It is a modern invention or an excuse. Fact: It has been described and researched for decades. Myth: It only affects children. Fact: Many adults have it undiagnosed, especially women. When to see a professional If symptoms affect school, work or relationships on an ongoing basis. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Multimodal intervention: psychoeducation, adjustments at school or work, behavioural therapy and, where appropriate, medication prescribed by a specialist. Tips for family and friends Build routines and break tasks into steps. Reward effort, not just results. Avoid negative labels (“lazy”, “scatterbrained”). Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/trastorn-espectre-autista.html","t":"Autism spectrum disorder (ASD)","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Neurodevelopment 🧩 Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) A different way of communicating, relating and perceiving the world. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Autism is a lifelong neurodevelopmental condition. Every person is different: support needs can vary widely. It is not an illness that needs to be “cured”. Common symptoms Differences in communication and social interaction Intense, specific interests Need for routine and difficulty with change Sensory sensitivity (noises, textures, lights) Myths and facts Myth: Autism can be cured with diets or vaccines. Fact: There is no evidence: vaccines do not cause autism. Myth: Autistic people have no emotions. Fact: They feel them, often very intensely, but may express them differently. When to see a professional If you suspect that you or someone close to you could be autistic, contact a specialist service for an assessment. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Autism is not treated as such; instead, support is offered: early intervention, speech therapy, educational and workplace adjustments and treatment of associated conditions (anxiety, depression). Tips for family and friends Respect their sensory and routine needs. Prepare them for changes in good time. Focus on strengths and interests. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/addiccions.html","t":"Addictions and problematic use","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Addictions 🔗 Addictions and problematic use Loss of control over substance use or behaviours (gambling, screens...). Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Addiction is a chronic, treatable illness. It is marked by difficulty controlling use despite negative consequences. Common symptoms Using more than planned or for longer Failed attempts to cut down or stop Tolerance and withdrawal Neglecting obligations and relationships Continuing despite the problems it causes Myths and facts Myth: You just need willpower to stop. Fact: Addiction affects brain circuits; it usually needs professional support. Myth: You have to “hit rock bottom” before asking for help. Fact: The sooner you ask for help, the better. When to see a professional If you feel you have lost control of your use or you are worried about someone close to you. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Individualised approach: outpatient care (CAS), motivational and cognitive behavioural therapy, mutual-help groups and, in some cases, medication or inpatient treatment. Tips for family and friends Speak out of concern, without reproach. Set clear limits and stick to them. Look for support for yourself in family groups. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/insomni.html","t":"Insomnia and sleep problems","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Sleep 🌙 Insomnia and sleep problems Difficulty sleeping that affects daily life. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Sleep and mental health are closely linked: sleeping badly can worsen mood and anxiety, and vice versa. Common symptoms Difficulty falling asleep or frequent waking Waking too early Tiredness during the day Irritability and difficulty concentrating Myths and facts Myth: You must always sleep 8 hours. Fact: Needs vary from person to person. Myth: Pills are the solution. Fact: For chronic insomnia, therapy is the first-line treatment. When to see a professional If insomnia lasts more than three nights a week for over a month. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is the recommended treatment. Sleep hygiene measures: regular schedules, avoiding screens and caffeine before bed, a dark, cool bedroom. Tips for family and friends Respect their schedules and rest. Avoid turning sleep into a source of pressure. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/dol.html","t":"Grief and loss","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Trauma and grief 🕯️ Grief and loss A natural process of reacting to a loss, which may need support. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Grief is the normal reaction to losing a loved one or an important situation. It has no timetable or rigid stages. Everyone experiences it in their own way. Common symptoms Sadness, longing, crying Anger, guilt, anxiety Difficulty concentrating, changes in sleep and appetite Feeling unreal or isolated Myths and facts Myth: You have to get over it within a set time. Fact: There is no deadline: every grief is unique. Myth: Crying is a sign of weakness. Fact: Emotional expression is part of healing. When to see a professional If after many months the pain is so intense that you cannot live normally, or you have thoughts of wanting to die to be reunited with the person you lost. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Support in grief groups, psychotherapy focused on complicated grief when needed, and social support. Tips for family and friends Support them with your presence, not with stock phrases. Talk about the person who has died if they wish. Offer practical help (meals, errands). Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/autolesions-suicidi.html","t":"Self-harm and suicidal thoughts","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Risk and crisis 🤝 Self-harm and suicidal thoughts If you are thinking of harming yourself right now, you are not alone: ask for help now. If you are in danger now Call 112 or 024 (free, 24 h). You don’t have to face this alone. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Suicidal thoughts and self-harm are forms of very intense emotional pain. They are more common than people think and they can be treated. You can feel better, even if it doesn’t seem so right now. Common symptoms Feeling there is no way out or being a burden Thoughts of death or of wanting to disappear Saying goodbye, giving away belongings, sudden changes in behaviour Self-harm as a way of coping with emotional pain Myths and facts Myth: Talking about it gives people ideas. Fact: Asking directly and calmly helps and does not increase the risk. Myth: People who talk about it don’t do it. Fact: Every comment must be taken seriously. When to see a professional If right now you are thinking of harming yourself or ending your life, call 112 (emergencies) or 024 (suicide prevention line, free, 24 h) now. If you can, don’t stay alone. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? Urgent or priority professional assessment, a safety plan, psychotherapy (CBT, DBT) and, if needed, medication. Recovery is possible. Tips for family and friends Ask directly and calmly: “Are you thinking of harming yourself?”. Listen without judging and don’t promise to keep it secret. Don’t leave them alone if there is immediate risk, and call 112. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/fobies.html","t":"Specific phobias","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Anxiety and related 🕷️ Specific phobias Intense, out-of-proportion fear of a specific thing or situation (animals, heights, blood, flying...). Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is A specific phobia is a very intense fear of a particular object or situation that almost always appears on encountering it and is clearly out of proportion to the real danger. The person avoids it or endures it with great distress. They are among the most common fears and respond best to treatment. Common symptoms Immediate fear or distress when faced with the trigger (or even just thinking about it) Palpitations, sweating, trembling, feeling breathless or dizzy Avoiding situations (flying, driving, going to the dentist, seeing blood...) Planning life so as not to meet what is feared Knowing the fear is exaggerated and still being unable to control it Myths and facts Myth: It is an exaggeration or a lack of willpower. Fact: Fear is an automatic response of the body. It is not solved by “trying harder”, but it is by gradual exposure. Myth: If I face it all at once, it will go away. Fact: Sudden exposure without preparation can reinforce the fear. It works better when gradual and agreed in advance. When to see a professional When avoidance limits work, studies, health (e.g. not going to the doctor) or relationships, or when the fear causes you significant distress. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? The treatment of choice is cognitive behavioural therapy with gradual exposure, often in just a few sessions. Medication is not usually the first resort. Tips for family and friends Don’t make fun of them or compare it to your own fear: for them it is real. Don’t force them to face it all at once, but don’t help them avoid everything either. Celebrate small steps forward and respect the pace of exposure. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/tept-complex.html","t":"Complex PTSD and ongoing trauma","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Trauma and grief 🧩 Complex PTSD and ongoing trauma Effects of repeated or prolonged trauma, often from childhood: beyond memories, it affects how you see yourself and relate to others. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Complex trauma refers to repeated or prolonged experiences that are hard to escape (abuse, neglect, violence). In addition to PTSD symptoms, ICD-11 describes complex PTSD with difficulty regulating emotions, a negative self-image and problems maintaining close relationships. Common symptoms Intrusive memories, nightmares or flashbacks Avoiding people, places or conversations that recall what happened Very intense emotions that are hard to calm, or feeling emotionally numb Persistent feelings of shame, guilt or of being “defective” Difficulty trusting others and keeping close bonds Feeling disconnected from your body or from reality (dissociation) Myths and facts Myth: If it happened years ago, I should be over it by now. Fact: Trauma doesn’t follow a calendar. Symptoms can appear or get worse many years later. Myth: If it had been that serious, I would remember everything clearly. Fact: It is common for memories of trauma to be fragmented or confused. That does not invalidate the experience. When to see a professional If the past shapes your present (relationships, sleep, emotions, substance use) or if your current safety is at risk. Look for a professional trained in trauma. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? A phased approach is recommended: first safety and stabilisation (emotion regulation, calming skills), then processing the trauma with trauma-focused therapies (trauma-focused CBT, EMDR) and, finally, reintegration into daily life. The pace is set by the person. Tips for family and friends Believe them and don’t press for details: they choose when and what to share. Help them feel safe through consistency and predictability. Learn about trauma: intense reactions are not “being dramatic”. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/ansietat-per-la-salut.html","t":"Health anxiety (hypochondria)","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Anxiety and related 🩺 Health anxiety (hypochondria) Persistent worry about having or being able to develop a serious illness, despite medical checks. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Health anxiety is when normal body sensations are interpreted as signs of a serious illness and the worry continues even though tests are normal. It is not “pretending to be ill”: the distress is real. It often leads to constant checking, searching online or asking for many appointments, or else to avoiding everything. Common symptoms Constant fear of having or developing a serious illness Excessive attention to bodily sensations (heartbeat, aches, marks) Checking your body, measuring vital signs or looking up symptoms online Repeatedly asking relatives or doctors for reassurance Avoiding news, hospitals or conversations about illness Brief relief after a check-up, then the fear returns Myths and facts Myth: It’s imagination: you don’t really feel anything. Fact: The sensations are real. The problem is how they are interpreted and the fear they cause. Myth: One more test will end the fear. Fact: Reassurance wears off quickly and feeds the cycle of checking. That is why treatment works on reducing it. When to see a professional When the worry takes up a lot of time, causes distress, interferes with daily life or leads to many visits or tests without relief. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? CBT is the best-evidenced treatment: reinterpreting sensations, reducing checking and reassurance-seeking. It can be combined with coordination between the family doctor and mental health services. An appropriate medical check is never ruled out. Tips for family and friends Listen without ridiculing, but avoid giving repeated reassurance: it reinforces the problem. Encourage them to have a single doctor they see regularly. Suggest activities that distract them and give them a full life. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/trastorns-del-son.html","t":"Other sleep disorders (apnoea, sleepiness, nightmares)","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Sleep 😴 Other sleep disorders (apnoea, sleepiness, nightmares) It isn’t all insomnia: apnoea, hypersomnia, narcolepsy, nightmares and parasomnias also affect rest. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Besides insomnia, there are other sleep problems worth knowing about. Obstructive sleep apnoea makes breathing stop briefly during the night. Hypersomnia and narcolepsy cause excessive daytime sleepiness. Repeated nightmares can appear with stress or trauma. Parasomnias (sleepwalking, night terrors) are more common in childhood. Many have a physical cause that needs to be assessed. Common symptoms Tiredness or sleepiness during the day despite sleeping many hours Loud snoring, pauses in breathing or waking up gasping Morning headaches, dry mouth Frequent nightmares that cause distress or make you avoid sleep Unusual movements or behaviour during sleep (walking, talking, shouting) Uncontrollable sleep attacks during the day Myths and facts Myth: Sleeping little is a sign of weak will or of being “lazy”. Fact: Sleep depends on health and physiology. Extreme tiredness can be a medical sign. Myth: Nightmares are just the result of a heavy dinner. Fact: They can be linked to stress, trauma or medication, and they can be treated. When to see a professional If daytime sleepiness makes it hard to drive, work or study, if someone sees you stop breathing while you sleep, or if nightmares or night-time episodes are frequent. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? It depends on the disorder. Apnoea is treated with lifestyle measures and, sometimes, positive-pressure devices (CPAP). A sleep study may help. For nightmares there are techniques such as imagery rehearsal therapy. A medical assessment is needed; sleep hygiene alone is not enough. Tips for family and friends If you notice pauses in breathing or loud snoring, encourage them to see a doctor. Avoid waking a person with parasomnias abruptly; protect them from bumps. Don’t joke about tiredness: it can be medical. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"},{"u":"trastorns/trastorns-alimentaris-per-edats.html","t":"Eating disorders across the lifespan","h":"What it is · Common symptoms · Myths and facts · When to see a professional · What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? · Tips for family and friends","x":"← All conditions Eating 🍎 Eating disorders across the lifespan How eating disorders can show up in childhood, adolescence, adulthood and older age. Information, not a diagnosis This page is for information only. Only a professional can assess your situation. What it is Eating disorders have no age. Although they are mostly detected in adolescence, they also appear in childhood, adulthood and older age, and often go unnoticed because they do not fit the usual picture. Spotting them early greatly improves the outlook. Common symptoms Childhood: very selective eating, fear of vomiting or choking, loss of appetite or weight, poor growth; may be ARFID (avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder) Adolescence: restriction and fear of gaining weight, binge eating, vomiting or laxatives, compulsive exercise, avoiding eating with the family, mood changes Adults: binge eating with loss of control and guilt, extreme diets, constant preoccupation with weight and body, secrecy; can coexist with work and family life Older adults: weight or appetite loss often put down to “age”, linked to loss, loneliness, depression or illness; risk of malnutrition At any age: obsession with weight and shape, isolation, changes in behaviour around food Myths and facts Myth: They only affect teenage girls. Fact: They affect people of all ages, genders and body types. You don’t have to be very thin to have an eating disorder. Myth: Young children who don’t eat “will eat when they’re hungry”. Fact: When eating is very selective or there is weight loss or intense fear, see a paediatrician. Myth: In older people, losing your appetite is normal. Fact: It can be a sign of depression, illness or an eating disorder and should be assessed. When to see a professional If there is notable weight or appetite loss, binge eating or vomiting, avoiding food, excessive concern about weight or changes in growth. For children and teenagers, see the paediatrician or GP soon. What treatments do clinical guidelines recommend? It depends on age: for teenagers with anorexia, family-based treatment (FBT) is the first choice. For adults, CBT and other psychotherapies, with medical and nutritional follow-up. For older people, a comprehensive medical assessment. A multidisciplinary team is often needed. Tips for family and friends Speak carefully and without blame: avoid commenting on weight or body. Keep family meals relaxed, without forcing or arguing. Look for professional help quickly; don’t wait for it to “pass”. Find help Self-help tools Sources for further reading: WHO – Mental health · Canal Salut (Government of Catalonia) · NICE clinical guidelines . Reference classifications: ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR. Professional review: pending. Content updated in November 2026. Who reviews it, and sources .","k":"f"}];
